'''''Pachygone laurifolia''''', the '''laurel-leaved snail tree''', is a medium-sized, shrubby evergreen tree of the moonseed family, [[Menispermaceae]]. It is native to the foothills of the Himalayas, China, Taiwan, and Japan,<ref>{{cite web |title=Flora of China |url=http://www.efloras.org/florataxon.aspx?flora_id=2&taxon_id=200008435 |publisher=efloras.org |accessdate=29 December 2018}}</ref> where it commonly grows to a height of {{cvt|7.5|ft|order=flip}}, with an equal spread. In cultivation it can reach a similar size where conditions are favorable.<ref>{{cite web |title=SelecTree: Tree Detail LAUREL LEAF COCCULUS |url=https://selectree.calpoly.edu/tree-detail/cocculus-laurifolius |website=SelecTree |publisher=Urban Forest Ecosystems Institute |accessdate=29 December 2018}}</ref> Form is round-headed, with a medium to fast growth rate and a coarse texture. Leaves are ovate-lanceolate, about {{cvt|6|in|order=flip}} long by {{cvt|2|in|order=flip}} wide, with a spiral bud arrangement. Leaf color is medium green. This species is [[dioecious]], with male and female flowers on separate plants. Flowers are insignificant: small, yellowish and appearing in spikes at [[leaf axil]]s.<ref>{{cite web |title=Cocculus laurifolius - Platter-Leaf |url=https://www.smgrowers.com/products/plants/plantdisplay.asp?plant_id=418 |website=San Marcos Growers |accessdate=29 December 2018}}</ref> Fruit is a small (6 mm) black [[drupe]]. With frequent shearing, plants may work well in a formal setting as a hedge or screen. Will take a wide range of sun and soil conditions within its temperature tolerance.

